notes:
Two bugs the user hit today, both in how an agent's tab (one held by a pane through the browser command or MCP) lives behind the screen.

1. Focus theft (urgent). The user is typing in their own input box, such as main's prompt or a pane, and the caret leaves it whenever an agent browses: open, go, click, type, screenshot or eval. An agent's tab must never take the keyboard from the window or move it within the window. Only the user's own action does that: the browser mode, a click on the page, or the focus verb from the screen. Find every path that does it. Candidates:
   - addChildView of a new WebContentsView in makeView (src/main/browser/browser-views.ts);
   - loadURL or navigation on a view;
   - input sent over the protocol (browserType, browserClick, browserPress in src/main/actions/browser/page.ts, through browser-page.ts);
   - capturePage;
   - keep() pushing browser:change to the renderer, then the renderer's browser view calling focusPage or focus on a state change (src/renderer/browser/browser-view.ts);
   - setVisible toggling in apply().
   Reproduce it first, with the steps written in the PR. Fix it at the root, in the one place every agent path goes through, and put the rule ("an agent's action never moves the keyboard") in a decision module with a test where it has branches. Record it as a hard rule in docs/browser.

2. Hidden tabs measure and behave wrongly. An agent's tab sits setVisible(false), so the page sees document.visibilityState 'hidden', rAF and timers are throttled, and nothing paints. A new-crm agent measuring performance found FCP recorded only at 31.6 s, when its screenshot forced a paint, and had to discard its paint metrics. An agent's tab should behave like a visible foreground tab at its size: visibilityState 'visible', painting, rAF and timers at full rate. It must still not be drawn over the user's screen or take focus. Options to evaluate: webContents.setBackgroundThrottling(false), and protocol Emulation.setFocusEmulationEnabled / Page.setWebLifecycleState, or keep the view 'visible' but off the window's drawn area or at zero opacity. Pick what really makes visibilityState visible and paint run, and verify it in the app with a page logging visibilityState and a PerformanceObserver for paint (data: URLs open now, since #372). Do this without a measurable cost when many tabs are open: only agent-held tabs, and only while held.

Update the dashboard-browser skill if what an agent can rely on changes, such as "paint metrics are valid in your tab". Never restart the running app. Check by hand in a dev build.
